  "missed": [
    {
      "point": "The levels are not flashbacks. A dream within a dream cannot be placed earlier than the one around it, so Nolan cuts between them as simultaneous strands tied together by shared deadlines.",
      "source": "https://www.davidbordwell.net/blog/?p=9770"
    },
    {
      "point": "The heist plan is not set out before the job begins. Exposition runs almost continuously, so the audience learns the plan alongside the team, at the cost of knowing less about the characters' backgrounds.",
      "source": "https://www.davidbordwell.net/blog/?p=9770"
    },
    {
      "point": "Earlier Nolan films intercut phases of one story. Inception intercuts embedded stories, and its climax juggles four nested timelines inside a fifth frame. Bordwell suggests that is near the limit of what viewers can follow.",
      "source": "https://www.davidbordwell.net/blog/?p=9770"
    }
  ],
